\par\par \b \uc2  CORRECTED -  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro upbeat, euro zone growth in focus. \b0\par\par\uc2 543 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Januar 2000\par 10:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2      In London story headlined "\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 -Euro upbeat, euro zone growth in focus", please read in 11th paragraph ...French PMI rose to a record 60.4 in December from 59.5 in November... \par \par \uc2  instead of ...French PMI rose to a record 62.6 in December from 62.4 in November. \par \par \uc2  A corrected repetition follows. By Shinichi Kishima \par \par \uc2  LONDON, Jan 3 (Reuters) - The euro was upbeat as it began its second year of trading on Monday, rising to two-week highs against the dollar before trimming gains in Europe amid growing optimism over an economic pickup in euro zone. With concerns of possible Y2K computer problems out of the way, focus was shifting to economic fundamentals, and the market was responding to bullish comments by European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials which it had mostly ignored in late 1999. \par \par \uc2  "It's quite surprising that they are seeing the euro-zone growth outpacing U.S. growth this year," said Eugen Keller, a market strategist at Dresdner Kleinwort Benson in Frankfurt, noting that the euro's long-term interest were rising. \par \par \uc2  ""Maybe this will open the eyes of \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers," he added. \par \par \uc2  The single European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose a cent to a two-week high of $1.0185 in thin Asian trading before pulling back in Europe, drawing support from ECB President Wim Duisenberg's comments on Sunday that the euro had potential to appreciate. \par \par \uc2  Duisenberg said in an interview on Dutch television he expected European growth this year to be faster than growth in the United States. \par \par \uc2  Early in European trading, Bank of France Governor Jean-Claude Trichet also said the euro had strong potential to appreciate. \par \par \uc2  Russian President Boris Yeltsin's surprise resignation last week was also expected to keep the euro supported. \par \par \uc2  "The fact that Yeltsin has gone at last is also seen as a bonus...the volatility and uncertainty associated with Russia could calm down," said Kevin Ralph, first vice president at Sanwa Bank in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  In addition, a flurry of European purchasing managers indices were seen supporting the euro. \par \par \uc2  French PMI rose to a record 60.4 in December from 59.5 in November. Italy's eased to 56.6 in December from November's 57.1 but it was the sixth straight month of readings above 50, which indicates expansion. Euro-zone PMI was due out at 0900 GMT. \par \par \uc2  "The euro can only ignore a stream of good data for so long before it has to respond," said Johnathan Bayley, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Paribas in Paris. With most traders in Tokyo and London still off for a public holiday on Monday, volume was still thin. But traders said activity was likely to pick up later in the day when U.S. traders return to work. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was broadly softer partly due to unwinding of year-end safe-haven buying, slipping to one-week lows below 102 yen against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Wariness of possible Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  cushioned dollar/yen's decline, traders said. While previous rounds of BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  have not clearly halted the dollar's decline, caution prevailed ahead of a Group of Seven deputy finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors meeting in Japan later this month. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 2000. \par \par \uc2 Dokumen