\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro hugs lows in early U.S. trade. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Lida Poletz \par \uc2 814 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Januar 2000\par 16:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Jan 3 (Reuters) - The European single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  clung to its lows against the dollar in early U.S. trade on Monday after a brief overnight rally fizzled, and traders expected Wall Street gains to keep the euro under pressure. \par \par \uc2  But the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  drifted down against the Japanese yen to within a quarter of a cent of 1999's four-year lows in thin, post-holiday trade. \par \par \uc2  "The themes from last year are still holding true this year," said Grant Wilson, vice president at Mellon Bank. \par \par \uc2  "There is still pressure on the euro, which I would expect to see make another run to parity. Meanwhile dollar/yen is sitting relatively close to lows from last year, and the question is will the (Bank of Japan) defend these levels." \par \par \uc2  Expectations that U.S. stocks would gain strongly in the first trading session of the new year was expected to reinforce the greenback's strength against the euro. The technology-heavy Nasdaq powered higher in early trade while the Dow Jones industrial average was slightly easier. Slumping U.S. bonds were less likely to affect the dollar, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "I would expect the euro to weaken and the dollar to do better because of stocks. Equities are what's been driving this market," said Tom Benfer, director at Bank of Montreal. \par \par \uc2  The market was also focused on data from the National Association of Purchasing Management for December 1999, which were released at 10 a.m. (1500 GMT). The U.S. Manufacturing Activity Index was 55.5 in December versus 56.2 in November. \par \par \uc2  The euro had started its second year of trading on a firm note, bolstered by hopes that Europe's growth could outpace U.S. growth this year, but failed to sustain the momentum despite strong European economic data. \par \par \uc2  At 9:25 a.m. (1425 GMT) the euro stood at $1.0063 against the dollar, virtually unchanged from Friday's U.S. close but down over a cent from two-week highs hit overnight. \par \par \uc2  European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Wim Duisenberg's comments on Sunday that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had the potential to appreciate had helped spark the brief rally. \par \par \uc2  What was worrying, traders said, was the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's tendency in recent months to ignore positive European economic data. The euro zone purchasing managers index rose to 57.4 in December, the highest in survey history, from 57.0 in November. \par \par \uc2  The data came after similar strong figures in German and French PMI data, which in turn followed comments earlier by Bank of France Governor Jean-Claude Trichet, who echoed Duisenberg's comments that the euro had the potential to appreciate. \par \par \uc2  But the dollar, at 101.54 yen, was down more than half a percent against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , partly due to unwinding of year-end safe-haven buying. \par \par \uc2  Still, wariness about possible Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  cushioned dollar/yen's decline, traders said. \par \par \uc2  While previous rounds of BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  have not clearly halted the dollar's decline, caution prevailed ahead of a Group of Seven deputy finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors meeting in Japan next week. \par \par \uc2  Traders expressed relief that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading appeared to be going on normally, with so sign of Y2K glitches. Trading had been thin overnight with most dealers in London and Tokyo off for public holidays. \par \par \uc2  "So far so good," Wilson said. "The banks are going to probably play it a bit slow starting off. We'll see how things play out and we'll probably return to normal mid-week." \par \par \uc2      ((U.S. Financial Markets Desk, 212 8859 1868)) \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 9:27 a.m. (1427 GMT).  All data taken \par \par \uc2  from Reuters with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. \par \par \uc2  close at 4:30 p.m. (2130 GMT). \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab \tab    Last     US Close  %Chg   YTD %     1999\par \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab 31 Dec.\tab \tab \tab  Close\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -------------------------------------------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Euro/dlr\tab \tab    1.0063   1.0070   -0.07   -0.07   1.0070\par Dlr/yen\tab \tab     101.54   102.21   -0.66   -0.66   102.21\par Euro/yen\tab \tab    102.18   103.10   -0.89   -0.89   103.10\par Dlr/swiss\tab \tab   1.5940   1.5902   +0.24   +0.24   1.5902\par Stg/dlr\tab \tab     1.6186   1.6176   +0.06   +0.06   1.6176\par Can/dlr\tab \tab     1.4415   1.4455   -0.28   -0.28   1.4455\par Aus/dlr\tab \tab     0.6578   0.6563   +0.23   +0.23   0.6563\par Euro/swiss\tab \tab  1.6050   1.6036   +0.09   +0.09   1.6036\par Euro/stg\tab \tab    0.6214   0.6239   -0.40   -0.40   0.6239\par  All \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  news ...\tab     Debt...   Indicators...\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reports..\tab \tab \tab \tab \par \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \par \par All spots\tab \par Tokyo spots\tab \par Europe spots\tab \par Volatilities\tab \par Tokyo \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market info from BOJ\tab \par World \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  news\tab \