\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro shoots higher as Wall Street stumbles. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Elizabeth Lazarowitz \par \uc2 813 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Januar 2000\par 19:06\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Jan 3 (Reuters) - Europe's single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rallied on the first full day of trading in 2000, jumping more than one percent as U.S. asset markets buckled when Wall Street staged a sharp U-turn from earlier gains. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Shortly after noon, the euro\tab   traded more than 1.25 percent firmer against the dollar from Friday's close at $1.02, having jumped 1-1/2 cents from Monday's lows alone.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  The buying occurred as "people are watching the equity markets have a turnaround," said Seth Garrett, global head of spot \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at Credit Suisse First Boston. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The euro also rose against the yen\tab      to trade more than a half percent firmer at 103.61 yen.\par The greenback also dipped against the yen\tab   amid the sharp stock-selloff, edging closer to four-year lows hit last year. The dollar then retraced some losses to stand nearly unchanged from the start of the New York session at 101.60 yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Ben Strauss, vice president at Bank Julius Baer and Co., agreed Wall Street was the fuel for the euro's sharp rise. "It's all equities," he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Shortly after noon, the Dow Jones industrial average was off nearly 1-1/2 percent, tumbling 168 points and erasing early gains put on as investors cheered 2000's nearly glitch-free start.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The technology-laden Nasdaq index had soared to all-time highs on Monday morning, but also turned negative by midday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "This looks like some very significant technical activity, (in stocks), which should help the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  a bit," Strauss forecast.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The British pound rose against the dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Weakness in U.S. Treasury bonds and a softer-than-expected reading from the National Association of Purchasing Management's closely watched manufacturing sector survey also dented the dollar, dealers said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par NAPM's PMI index eased to 55.5 in December from 56.2 in November, showing manufacturing expanded at a slower pace.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The data contrasted with more positive economic figures from Europe that came overnight. The euro zone purchasing managers index rose to 57.4 in December, the highest in survey history, from 57.0 in November.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "What you see is a signal that (in Europe) manufacturing confidence, at least, has completely caught up with the U.S. and has at this point, moved beyond it," said Jim McCormick, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at J.P. Morgan.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The euro started its second year of trading on a firm note, bolstered by positive data and comments from European officials, which lifted hopes that Europe's growth could outpace U.S. growth this year.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  President Wim Duisenberg's comments on Sunday that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  had the potential to appreciate helped spark the brief rally.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Strong German and French PMI data, as well as earlier comments by Bank of France Governor Jean-Claude Trichet, who echoed Duisenberg's comments that the euro had the potential to appreciate, helped boost the year-old \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In trading against the yen, the dollar remained down about a half percent from its prior close, but fears about possible Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  cushioned the dollar's decline against the yen, traders said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par While previous rounds of BOJ \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have not clearly halted the dollar's decline, caution prevailed ahead of a meeting of Group of Seven deputy finance ministers and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governors in Japan next week.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Traders expressed relief that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trading appeared to be going on normally, with so sign of Y2K glitches. Trading had been thin overnight, with most dealers in London and Tokyo off for public holidays.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "So far so good," said Grant Wilson, a trader at Mellon Bank in Pittsburgh. "The banks are probably going to play it a bit slow starting off. We'll see how things play out and we'll probably return to normal midweek."\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bid prices at 1803GMT.  All data taken from\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Reuters with percent change calculated from the daily U.S.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par close at 2130GMT.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab \tab    Last     US Close  %Chg   YTD %  1999 Cls\par \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab 31 Dec.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -------------------------------------------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Euro/dlr\tab \tab    1.0196   1.0070   +1.25   +1.25   1.0070\par Dlr/yen\tab \tab     101.72   102.21   -0.48   -0.48   102.21\par Euro/yen\tab \tab    103.67   103.10   +0.55   +0.55   103.10\par Dlr/swiss\tab \tab   1.5739   1.5902   -1.03   -1.03   1.