\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro stagnant within sight of parity. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Shinichi Kishima \par \uc2 499 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Dezember 1999\par 09:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Dec 29 (Reuters) - The euro struggled to move away from the psychologically important one-for-one level against the dollar on Wednesday, reluctant to put up a fight against the upbeat U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ahead of the year-end. \par \par \uc2  The euro treaded water in a narrow range within one cent of the parity level, failing to show any resilience after Tuesday's mild losses, due in part to a rise in the Dow Jones Industrial Average to a record close, but finding no follow-through selling either. \par \par \uc2  "We're already dealing for the new year but there's not a great deal of activity," said Nick Parsons, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Commerzbank in London. "The range so far has been barely 20 points (0.2 cent) overnight, and it's very difficult to see anything that's going to change that pattern." Spot \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  transactions traded on Wednesday will settle in January, and traders were wary of carrying settlement risk over the year-end due to Y2K concerns. In addition to the technical issues, the euro has been trading within one cent either side of $1.01 for over a week, which has made short-term momentum-followers disinterested in trading for now. \par \par \uc2  The market showed negligible reaction to comments by European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Vice President Christian Noyer in French newspaper Liberation that the euro had strong potential to appreciate and he was personally not happy with the euro's fall. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the market was becoming immune to such remarks and was unlikely to believe the ECB would take any imminent actions to reverse the euro's year-long weakness. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen was also stuck in a narrow range above 102 yen as the 102-area had proved to be good support in the last few days amid wariness of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the yen's export-crippling ascent. \par \par \uc2  "Nobody wants to get far from the 102.00-102.50 yen range today," said a Japanese trust bank dealer in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said that although activity was likely to dry up as the year-end approached, the BOJ could step into the market whenever thin volume exaggerated price fluctuations, particularly a drop in the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "The BOJ seems to be prepared for unexpected \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements, especially today and tomorrow. That's because, for example, one exporter sell order could result in a sudden drop amid a lack of participants," said a U.S. bank dealer in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ's resolve to counter any Y2K-related disruptions was apparent in a record 14.6 trillion yen surplus it left in the Japanese money market on Tuesday, up from an already extraordinary 9.1 trillion on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  But since the surplus was seen as a one-off event and not a signal of further credit easing, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market showed negligible reaction to the BOJ's money market \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dvct07k9o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
