\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Buoyant yen cedes some ground in thin trade. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Shinichi Kishima \par \uc2 594 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Dezember 1999\par 12:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Dec 28 (Reuters) - The broadly buoyant yen ceded some ground on Tuesday after briefly firming on sporadic year-end commercial demand as wariness of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  capped follow-through buying. \par \par \uc2  The to-and-fro movement was amplified by reluctance among price-making banks to take on fresh positions ahead of the year-end due to Y2K concerns, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "It's only some commercial interest, nothing more," said Eugen Keller, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and fixed income strategist at Dresdner Kleinwort Benson in Frankfurt. "Speculative accounts, trading accounts are probably closed now." \par \par \uc2  An absence of actual BOJ yen selling, expectations the Japanese economy would continue to recover next year and that Tokyo stocks would extend gains initially supported the yen, taking dollar/yen to a session low near 102 yen early in Europe from the highs in Asia around 102.70. \par \par \uc2  By European midsession, however, the dollar had recovered toward the middle of that range, which was also the middle of a relatively tight 101.20-103.80 monthly range for December. \par \par \uc2  Michael Rottmann, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at HypoVereinsbank in Munich, said the dollar's range-trading with some downward bias was likely to continue until the next meeting of Group of Seven finance ministers to be held in Japan in January. \par \par \uc2  "We still believe that the low end of the range is somewhere between 101 and 102, more or less based on fear of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and the upper end should be between 103 and 104," he said. \par \par \uc2  Earlier in the day, the yen weathered the BOJ's decision to leave a staggering 9.1 trillion net surplus in the Japanese money market, more than twice Monday's 4.5 trillion yen, which itself was a record. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ said the surplus was boosted to prevent Y2K pressures from inflating the key overnight rate, which it has long kept around 0.02 percent. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo dealers said the huge surplus blurred the argument over "unsterilised \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ", in which the BOJ leaves extra yen funds in the private sector resulting from its \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in order to subdue demand for yen. The BOJ has long argued such a policy is meaningless. \par \par \uc2  Indeed, dealers found it hard to see what difference it would make by leaving Friday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  unsterilised when the transactions settled today, as it probably amounted to a couple of billion yen amid an overall surplus of nine trillion. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is small beer," said a dealer at a European bank. "You can't even spot it amongst all this liquidity." \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the euro was stuck in a very tight band above $1.01 and traders saw little prospect of a breakout given the London market was still off for a bank holiday. \par \par \uc2  Nevertheless, the euro's sentiment was underpinned by the positive outlook for the German share market after last week's gains on a government proposal to abolish a tax on company sales of cross shareholdings. \par \par \uc2  European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  directorate member Tommaso Padoa-Schioppa was quoted by Germany weekly Die Welt as saying clear signs of structural reforms in Germany and Italy would help the euro strengthen. \par \par \uc2  European economic data were scarce, and a downward revision in October's pan-German output index to 107.4 from 107.6 triggered virtually no reaction in the euro exchange rates. Euro-zone money supply was in line with market expectations, with 12-month average M3 rising 6.2 percent in November against a 5.8-percent gain in October. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dvcs07jbd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
