\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Yen firmer, BOJ's impact seen subsiding. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Shinichi Kishima \par \uc2 613 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Dezember 1999\par 13:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Dec 27 (Reuters) - The yen crept firmer on Monday, despite wariness of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to halt its export-crippling ascent, as the market remained unconvinced that the BOJ's yen selling last week would turn the trend around. \par \par \uc2  Although surprise BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last Friday kept traders nervous about further \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action, a lack of significant follow-through yen-selling by the private sector left underlying yen bullishness intact, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "What is clear is that the BOJ by itself will not turn around the dollar/yen move," said Gilles Borrel, head of treasury sales at Westdeutsche Landesbank in Paris. \par \par \uc2  The only flows in the market appeared to be Japanese exporters' selling in euro/yen and dollar/yen to transfer foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  receipts into yen before the year-end, with its impact amplified by low liquidity due to a London holiday and Y2K concerns, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "Bank dealers are there today only to execute customer orders," said Akira Hoshino, chief trader at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi in London. \par \par \uc2  "Those orders are getting scarce, but when they come, dealers just have to turn around to the interbank market and hit whatever the bid or offer that's out there because they can't take positions right now." \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen was below 102.50 yen in afternoon Europe, nearly a yen off Friday's post-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  peak. Euro/yen was also near session lows around 103.70 yen. \par \par \uc2  The yen did not budge even after the BOJ left a huge 4.5 trillion yen in the Japanese money market, the largest daily surplus ever, as traders did not see this as a signal of an easier monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ itself portrayed the huge surplus as a response to Y2K pressures, as commercial banks coped with heavy customer demand for cash before the year-end. \par \par \uc2  On Sunday, former Finance Ministry \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  guru Eisuke Sakakibara tried to resurrect the case for "unsterilised \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ", saying the BOJ could weaken the yen by leaving the funds from Friday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the money market. \par \par \uc2  But Tokyo dealers noted the surplus was already so large that it made the argument largely academic. \par \par \uc2  "The BOJ bought maybe a billion dollars on Friday, certainly not more than two," said a dealer at a U.S. bank. \par \par \uc2  "If it leaves it in (the money market), that's one to two hundred billion yen against a surplus today of 4.5 trillion. How would you even know?" \par \par \uc2  In a normal economy, unsterilised \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  works in part by putting downward pressure on interest rates, so making it less attractive for investors to hold the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Finance Ministry officials still tried to make Friday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  more cost effective by talking up the threat today. \par \par \uc2  But BOJ Governor Masaru Hayami said the yen's rise reflected increased overseas confidence in Japan's recovery. \par \par \uc2  A Ministry of International Trade and Industry official also said a 3.8 percent seasonally-adjusted rise in Japan's November industrial output showed there was so far no clear sign of damage to the Japanese industry caused by a stronger yen. \par \par \uc2  The euro was little changed against the dollar, drifting inside last week's tight ranges. It briefly tested session lows near $1.0110 when euro/yen selling spilt over but managed to hold that level, which chartists saw as key support. \par \par \uc2  The euro showed negligible reaction to Germany's producer price index for November released early in the session. \par \par \uc2  German PPI was up 0.1 percent month-on-month and up 0.7 percent year-on-year, largely in line with expectations. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dvcr07ine\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
