\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Yen buoyant but hesitates to attack peaks. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Shinichi Kishima \par \uc2 535 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Dezember 1999\par 09:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Dec 23 (Reuters) - The yen remained broadly firm on Thursday but hesitated to test lifetime highs against the euro and four-year peaks against the dollar amid dwindling liquidity due to the year-end and a Tokyo holiday. The buoyant Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was confined to narrow ranges near the top of recent ranges amid a lack of speculative interest to counter or latch onto light customer orders which had driven it toward the crucial peaks this week, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "The yen continues to press down toward the huge psychological level of 100 against the dollar and 100 against the euro, but I don't think we're going to see too much further move at this point," said Jeremy Stretch, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at NatWest Global Financial Markets in London. \par \par \uc2  In early European trading, the euro was adrift within about a quarter yen of historic lows near 102 yen set in early December, while the dollar was within a quarter yen of four-year lows near 101.20 yen set in late November. \par \par \uc2  The thin year-end market conditions, exacerbated by a Japanese holiday, meant the risk was for a test of those levels unless recent commercial order flows reversed, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "We have seen a lot of Japanese exporters repatriate funds during the course of the last few weeks, and that still is a possible factor certainly prior to the year-end," Stretch at NatWest said. "If we do see any yen buying of any size, that could, on what are exceptionally thin markets, provide some upward bias for the yen for those critical levels." Expectation that Japanese equities would continue to look attractive next year added to the yen's bullishness, particularly after Wednesday's 2.1-percent rally in the Nikkei average, although wariness of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  discouraged speculative yen buying for now, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "I think if the Nikkei moves above 19,000, dollar/yen could break 100," said Matthew Clements, economist at Prebon Marshall Yamane in London. "Most people are expecting it to happen at one point, but the general consensus is that a stronger yen would not be tolerated by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , so there might be some form of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  The euro gained a bit of ground against the dollar but remained well within its recent tight range near $1.01. \par \par \uc2  Reluctance to take fresh positions kept the euro from moving much either way on economic data, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "No one's doing anything at the moment. It's all on a need-to-do basis. No one is doing anything that'll put on a risk; they're doing everything to reduce it," said Robert Russell, deputy treasurer at Fuji Bank in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  France reported a 0.3-percent fall in the nation's consumer spending for November against a 2.2-percent revised gain in October. France's November final consumer price index was unchanged month-on-month and up 0.9 percent on the year. \par \par \uc2  The German state of Hesse reported its December CPI rose 0.2 percent month-on-month and 1.5 percent year-on-year. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dvcn07qfh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
