\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro drifts off highs as market's energy ebbs. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Lida Poletz \par \uc2 888 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Dezember 1999\par 19:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Dec 23 (Reuters) - The European single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trimmed its gains against the dollar in quiet afternoon dealing on Thursday after an early upside scramble ran out of steam. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The euro\tab   had jumped nearly a cent to a high of $1.0180 and traders said large European orders had triggered short covering as sell stops were hit in thin, choppy trade.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  "It looks like there (were) some big orders going through.... Somebody was a lot shorter than they wanted to be in Europe," said Peter Lorraine, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Brown Brothers Harriman. \par \par \uc2  But momentum rapidly ebbed and the euro edged back from its highs to $1.1045 by 1 p.m. (1800 GMT), which was up around half a percent on the day. \par \par \uc2  Overall volumes were very light and traders reported precious little business going through, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "It's practically Christmas, Tokyo was closed overnight for the Emperor's birthday and part of Europe is getting ready to close down tomorrow," said Hillel Waxman, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at Bank Leumi USA in New York. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar\tab   also erased modest early gains against the Japanese yen after a short-lived uptick to 102 yen - a move that followed the yen's brief dip against the euro. It was virtually unchanged from Wednesdays' close near 101.75 yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Analysts said the yen's strength remained intact and would continue into next year.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The yen posted solid gains earlier this week amid expectations of flows into Japanese assets in 2000 and targeted the 101.22 level reached on November 26, the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's weakest since December, 1995.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "There are good reasons fundamentally for the yen to be strong," said David Coleman, chief economist at CIBC World Markets in London. "The weakness of U.S. bonds has also tended to take the shine off the dollar," he added.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Expectations of a Japanese economic recovery have enticed foreign investors into Japanese stocks this year. The Nikkei average rallied by 2.1 percent on Wednesday, while U.S. Treasuries closed, by contrast, near two-year lows.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Some analysts said the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  might attempt verbal or direct \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  over the Christmas and New Year holiday period. The Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at least a dozen times since June to curb the yen's strength, but has failed to stop an 18 percent rally in the Japanese unit against the dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But U.S. traders said such concerns were probably unfounded between now and the year end: "It's the end of the year, there's no one around and there are fears about thinness and Y2K. This is not the time for banks to come in," Waxman said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Strong gains in U.S. stocks had little impact on the dollar, even as the Dow Jones industrial average powered to record intra-day highs above 11,400 points.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Data showing that Americans' incomes rose and new orders for costly durable goods rebounded strongly in November also failed to excite the dollar. Analysts said the data simply reinforced expectations the U.S. Federal Reserve will raise interest rates next year.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "This is more of the same," said Stan Shipley, senior economist at Merrill Lynch. "The Fed will probably tighten, we anticipate in February - not because of inflation....but because growth is too strong."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By contrast, traders said positive euro-zone data overnight had helped spur the euro's gains. Germany's preliminary consumer prices in December rose 0.2 percent month-on-month.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par German stocks also rose on a government proposal to eliminate the tax on firm-to-firm sales of shareholdings in a move to boost German industry restructuring. Analysts said the plan, if it becomes law as proposed in 2001 could unleash a wave of mergers and restructuring.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The CPI data was higher than expected, which can lead to some talk of tightening by the ECB," said Mike Malpede, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Refco Group. "But probably more importantly...if the tax law changes, it's probably good for business in Euroland."\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bid prices at 1803GMT.  All data taken from Reuters\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par with percent change calculated from the daily U.S.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par close at 2130GMT.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab \tab    Last     US Close  %Chg   YTD %  1998 Cls\par \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab 22 Dec.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -------------------------------------------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Euro/dlr\tab \tab    1.0144   1.0094   +0.50  -13.45   1.1721\par Dlr/yen\tab \tab     101.74   101.68   +0.06  -10.12   113.20\par Euro/yen\tab \tab    103.20   102.53   +0.65  -21.72   131.84\par Dlr/swiss\tab \tab   1.5801   1.5855   -0.34  +15.04   1.3735\par Stg/dlr\tab \tab     1.6144   1.6080   +0.