\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro whisks higher versus dollar in thin trade. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Lida Poletz \par \uc2 892 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Dezember 1999\par 16:56\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Dec 23 (Reuters) - The European single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  whooshed higher against the dollar in early U.S. trade on Thursday as wafer-thin holiday trade exaggerated price action. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In whippy trading, the euro\tab   jumped sharply to a session high of $1.0180, up over a cent from overnight lows. Large European orders triggered waves of short covering after sell stops were hit at $1.0115 and $1.0140, traders said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  "It looks like there are some big orders going through," said Peter Lorraine, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Brown Brothers Harriman. "It looks like somebody was a lot shorter than they wanted to be in Europe." \par \par \uc2  But overall trading volumes were very light and traders reported precious little business going through. \par \par \uc2  "It's practically Christmas, Tokyo was closed overnight for the Emperor's birthday and part of Europe is getting ready to close down tomorrow," said Hillel Waxman, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at Bank Leumi USA in New York. "Effectively the market has moved into a state of twiddling its thumbs." \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Meanwhile the dollar firmed modestly versus the Japanese yen\tab  , which had gained strongly earlier this week amid expectations of flows into Japanese assets in 2000.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The greenback traded up to 101.80 yen, edging away from a recent downside target - the 101.22 level reached on Nov. 26, the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's weakest since December, 1995.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But the yen's strength remained intact, and would continue into next year, analysts said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "There are good reasons fundamentally for the yen to be strong," said David Coleman, chief economist at CIBC World Markets in London." The weakness of U.S. bonds has also tended to take the shine off the dollar," he added.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Expectations of a Japanese economic recovery have enticed foreign investors into Japanese stocks this year. The Nikkei average rallied 2.1 percent to close at 18,462 on Wednesday, while U.S. Treasuries, in contrast, closed near two-year lows.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Some analysts said the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  might attempt verbal or direct \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  over the Christmas and New Year holiday period. The Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at least a dozen times since June to curb the yen's strength, but has failed to stop an 18 percent rally in the Japanese unit against the dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But U.S. traders said such concerns were probably unfounded between now and the year end: "It's the end of the year, there's no one around and there are fears about thinness and Y2K. This is not the time for banks to come in," Waxman said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Data showing that Americans' incomes rose in November and new orders for costly durable goods rebounded strongly failed to have much impact in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Analysts said the data reinforced expectations the U.S. Federal Reserve is poised to raise interest rates next year.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "This is more of the same," said Stan Shipley, senior economist at Merrill Lynch. "The Fed will probably tighten, we anticipate in February - not because of inflation....but because growth is too strong."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By contrast, traders said positive euro-zone data overnight had helped spur the euro's session gains.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Germany reported that its December preliminary consumer prices rose by 0.2 percent month-on-month and 1.1 percent year-on-year. Average 1999 CPI was up 0.6 percent compared with 1998, the lowest rise since 1991.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par German stocks also rose on a government proposal to eliminate the tax on firm-to-firm sales of shareholdings in a move to boost German industry restructuring. Analysts said the plan, if it becomes law as proposed in 2001, could unleash a wave of mergers and restructuring.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The CPI data was higher than expected, which can lead to some talk of tightening by the ECB," said Mike Malpede, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Refco Group. "But probably more importantly, the Germans announced a plan to allow banks to divest some of their assets without paying tax. If the tax law changes, it's probably good for business in Euroland."\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bid prices at 10:34 a.m. (1534 GMT).  All data taken\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par from Reuters with percent change calculated from the daily U.S.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par close at 4:30 p.m. (2130 GMT).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab \tab    Last     US Close  %Chg   YTD %    1998\par \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab 22 Dec.\tab \tab \tab  Close\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -------------------------------------------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Euro/dlr\tab \tab    1.0157   1.0094   +0.62  -13.34   1.1721\par Dlr/yen\tab \tab     101.79   101.68   +0.11  -10.08   113.20\par Euro/yen\tab \tab    103.35   102.53   +0.80  -21.61