\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Yen soars, eyes historic peaks vs euro. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Shinichi Kishima \par \uc2 578 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Dezember 1999\par 14:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par LONDON, Dec 22 (Reuters) - The yen soared towards historic peaks against the euro\tab      and four-year highs against the dollar  on Wednesday, fuelled by expectations of continued global fund flows into Japan next year.\par The yen's strength was reignited by a powerful 2.1-percent rally in the Tokyo stock market's Nikkei average\tab   , which traders said was a reminder of the main themes of second half 1999 - Japan's economic recovery and foreign investors' long-awaited return to Japanese equities.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Our view here is that we're still in the midst of a very strong yen uptrend, and our target for the first quarter (2000) is 90 for dollar/yen," said Steve Barrow, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Bear Stearns in London.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell as low as 101.57 yen, within a half yen of four-year lows near 101.20 set in late November, while the euro dropped to 102.34 yen, approaching life lows near 102 touched in early December. \par \par \uc2  The buoyant Nikkei took its cue from equally exuberant U.S. technology sector stocks, while the broader U.S. stock and bond markets were relatively subdued despite the Federal Reserve's decision on Tuesday to keep its monetary policy unchanged. \par \par \uc2  The Fed left interest rates steady and maintained a neutral stance after Tuesday's policy meeting but made clear in a statement issued afterwards that it was still concerned about inflationary pressures. \par \par \uc2  "They didn't want to rock the boat before the millennium, but the (Fed's) statement shows that they're obviously still looking to hike rates next year," said Sonja Hellemann, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Paribas Capital Markets in London. \par \par \uc2  Beside the stock market, traders said there were steady commercial orders, mainly from Japanese exporters and investors, which also helped push up the yen. \par \par \uc2  "I think foreign investors' yen asset buying will continue next year, as will Japanese exporters' desperate dollar selling and a slowdown in Japanese investors' foreign investment," said Junpei Yamamoto, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi in London. \par \par \uc2  Wariness of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept the market jittery, but a Japanese holiday on Thursday was seen reducing the chances of an imminent \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action. \par \par \uc2  In addition, reports of Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa's sanguine comment about the threat of a higher yen to the Japanese economy accelerated the yen's rally in mid-morning Europe. Japan's Jiji Press quoted Miyazawa as saying he had no idea about the dollar's fall below 102 yen. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Meanwhile, the euro was little changed against the dollar\tab  , hovering near $1.01 - the middle of a week-long range.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Some analysts saw a chance that the euro would stage a rebound after its recent hapless performance due in part to the year-end factors.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I think it will hover around $1.01, but because the market has been so short of euro heading into the year-end, the risk is on the upside," said Will Rugg, a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  analyst at S&P MMS International in London.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On the other hand, Barrow at Bear Stearns said recent European prices data, including a flurry of consumer prices data for German states released on Wednesday, had shown signs of a pickup in the area's inflationary pressures, which could dog the single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  next year.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ((London Capital Markets +44-171-542-6721, shinichi.kishima@reuters.com)).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1999.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dvcm07tqs\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
