\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro dips to 1-week low vs dlr, parity close. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Carolyn Cohn \par \uc2 615 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Dezember 1999\par 14:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Dec 15 (Reuters) - The euro fell to a one-week low within a quarter cent of the key one-to-one level with the dollar on Wednesday, as traders eyed U.S. data for evidence of strong but non-inflationary growth. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was benefiting from Tuesday's retail sales and consumer price data which showed growth in the U.S. economy remained strong with no signs of inflation. The market was now turning its attention to U.S. industrial production and capacity utilisation data for November, due at 1415 GMT, and to Thursday's trade data and Philadelphia Fed survey. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the data may surprise on the stronger side of expectations. \par \par \uc2  "Industrial production is a key focus," said David Brickman, international economist at PaineWebber. \par \par \uc2  "U.S. data are eventually going to show a slowdown in growth, but yesterday's retail sales data were particularly strong." \par \par \uc2  Industrial production is forecast to rise by 0.2 percent in November from the previous month, and by 0.7 percent year-on-year. Capacity utilisation is forecast at 80.6 percent, from the previous 80.7 percent. \par \par \uc2  In thin year-end trading conditions, the dollar came close to two-week highs against the yen set on Tuesday, and to 10-year peaks set against the Swiss franc earlier this month. \par \par \uc2  "The market's inclination at the moment is to stick with the trend and for euro/dollar this is down," said Steve Barrow, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bear Stearns International in London. \par \par \uc2  "The market is not necessarily moving that much on fundamental news and rather getting direction from people adjusting their positions." \par \par \uc2  Traders said the euro unwound gains made earlier in the European session on the back of buying by French and German banks. \par \par \uc2  ECB HOLDS RATES STEADY \par \par \uc2  The euro failed to find any joy in better-than-expected German wholesale prices, which rose 3.2 percent in November, the biggest increase for more than two years. \par \par \uc2  The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  left its refinancing rate steady at 3.0 percent, as expected, following its final policy-setting meeting of the year. \par \par \uc2  The euro was trading around $1.0020 at 1247 GMT, around a third of a cent above lifetime lows of $0.9986 set on December 6. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen was just above 103.50, against Tuesday's two-week highs of 103.76, up 2.5 percent from four-year lows set in late November at 101.22. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the yen was undermined by the continued negative undertone to the Nikkei and by the expectation that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb yen strength on further sharp falls in dollar/yen. Dealers said the yen was also suffering a little from recent disappointing Japanese economic data. \par \par \uc2  The drachma edged briefly higher against the euro in a relief rally after the Bank of Greece cut interest rates by 75 basis points, as expected. \par \par \uc2  The Greek \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut its key 14-day deposit rate to 10.75 percent and its Lombard rate to 12.25 percent, saying it believed core inflation and average European Union harmonised inflation rates were headed downwards, despite recent increases in headline inflation. \par \par \uc2  The rate cuts fuelled expectations Greece will revalue the drachma ahead of entering the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The euro was trading at around 330 drachmas at 1247 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Sterling, meanwhile, rose only briefly against the dollar after British unemployment fell to a 19-year low of 4.1 percent, before the British \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bowed to general dollar strength. Sterling was trading a little above earlier one-week lows of $1.6040. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dvcf085rb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
