\par\par \b \uc2  Sakakibara: Japan Likely To Stop Dlr-Yen Drop Below Y100 \b0\par\par\uc2 407 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Dezember 1999\par 03:48\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)-  Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely prevent a fall in the dollar to below Y100, the former vice-finance minister for international affairs said Monday. \par \par \uc2  In a speech in Tokyo, Eisuke Sakakibara said, "Japan should prevent the dollar-yen from falling below Y100 and (Prime Minister Keizo) Obuchi and (Finance Minister Kiichi) Miyazawa both have the political will to do this." \par \par \uc2  Regarding possible joint-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the U.S. and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, Sakakibara said market participants should not rule out such a possibility. \par \par \uc2  "Joint \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  occur based on the market situation. The market should not consider this as absolutely impossible," he said. \par \par \uc2  "If the (\b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) market becomes disruptive, the U.S. will take action, although I'm not sure what kind of action will be taken." \par \par \uc2  The U.S. authorities do not desire a significant fall in the dollar, especially a fall below Y100, he noted. \par \par \uc2  Regarding the recent weakness in the euro against the dollar and the yen, Sakakibara said he expected the European single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to eventually rebound. \par \par \uc2  "It is common perception that European nations desire a strong euro. I think the euro's weakness is a temporary phenomenon. I doubt the trend of the euro weakening will continue." \par \par \uc2  Regarding remarks by Bank of Japan governor Masaru Hayami in parliament Friday that current dollar-yen levels are acceptable, Sakakibara said: "His remarks were irresponsible and he should be more responsible when he says something. If he can't do this, he should resign". \par \par \uc2  Regarding the negative growth in gross domestic product for the July-September quarter, Sakakibara said GDP growth for the current fiscal year will likely be more than 1% despite the negative figure for the July-September quarter, as he expects the figures for the October-December and January-March quarters will not be bad. \par \par \uc2  "There is a high possibility that economic growth for the current fiscal year will be above 1%," he said. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara also said the U.S. economy will likely remain stable, and as the result the U.S. stock market won't experience a significant drop. \par \par \uc2  "While I am not saying the Dow will edge higher above 12000 or 13000 levels, fear of a significant drop in the Dow is receding," he said. \par \par \uc2  He said the Asian economies have been recovering steadily, which will have positive effects on Japan's domestic economy. \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dvc608b2q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
