\par\par \b \uc2  Trichet, Miyazawa did not talk  \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention \highlight\uc2  - MOF. \b0\par\par\uc2 272 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Dezember 1999\par 11:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Dec 6 (Reuters) - Bank of France chief Jean-Claude Trichet and Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa did not discuss the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  during a meeting on Monday, a Japanese ministry official said. \par \par \uc2  The official, briefing reporters on the 30-minute meeting, said the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not come up and that Trichet did not discuss euro policy. \par \par \uc2  Trichet, leaving the ministry, declined comment on the whether \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was discussed, saying only: "We discussed all matters." \par \par \uc2  The official said Trichet and Miyazawa had a casual discussion on the Japanese and European economies and a seminar in Tokyo on Monday attended by Trichet and Miyazawa's deputy Haruhiko Kuroda. \par \par \uc2  He said the two also did not discuss who should be the next managing director of the International Monetary Fund, a post for which Japan is pushing former Finance Ministry official Eisuke Sakakibara but which traditionally goes to a European. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda told Monday's seminar the euro was too weak and that stronger cooperation among various countries might be needed to minimise \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility. \par \par \uc2  Trichet told the seminar the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , of which he is a Governing Council member, was pursuing a strong euro policy, but the European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slid to a fresh record low against the yen while he was speaking. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  twice last week, selling yen for dollars and euros, but the U.S. and European authorities have shown no inclination to help Tokyo boost the euro and weaken the yen. ((William Mallard, Tokyo newsroom +81-3 3432-8022 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dvc608q2w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
