\par\par \b \uc2  Japan should try to keep dlr over Y100-Sakakibara. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,147 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Dezember 1999\par 06:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Dec 6 (Reuters) - Former Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Eisuke Sakakibara said on Monday that Japan's monetary authorities should make every effort to prevent the dollar from slipping below 100 yen. \par \par \uc2  He also told a seminar this would be possible through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  alone. \par \par \uc2  "The market thinks joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is the only way to prevent the dollar's fall, but I think \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  alone would be effective," he said. \par \par \uc2  "I told (my successor Haruhiko) Kuroda that conducting $100 billion worth of dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the end of March would be effective, and said 'Why not do it?'," he added. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  received permission from the cabinet in November to raise more funds in the short-term money market to use to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  The ceiling on borrowing in the ministry's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  special account was lifted to 49 trillion yen from 39 trillion yen as part of the second supplementary budget for the fiscal year to next March. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara also said excessive declines in the dollar against the yen would damage the U.S. economy more than they would damage the Japanese economy. \par \par \uc2  He said there was no guarantee there would not be joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. "The market should be cautious (about joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) in December, when market liquidity is reduced. The market now fears a sudden fall in the dollar amid a thin market, but actually it is also a good chance (for the monetary authorities) to lift the dollar up." \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara also said he believed Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa would decisively defend the dollar against a fall below 100 yen. \par \par \uc2  On the recent fall in the euro below parity against the dollar, he said this was a short-term trend and the euro would strengthen in the medium to longer term. "I don't know by how much Japanese investors are cutting their losses by selling the euro but it would be very stupid for them to cut losses (on their euro holdings) at this point. If it were me, I would be very patient." \par \par \uc2  He said he was confident about the euro-zone economy because the information technology revolution, which had led the U.S. economy's growth, would start permeating in Japan and Europe. \par \par \uc2  On market perceptions about confusing comments by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Sakakibara said he saw it as a gap between the French and German approaches to making monetary policy, but he thought this would adjust gradually. \par \par \uc2  "What is more confusing is the Bank of Japan's (monetary policy-making). It's the worst." \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara said Hayami told parliament last week he was comfortable with the current dollar/yen rate. "That is a total mistake... Someone who cannot be responsible about he says should step down from his position." \par \par \uc2  The BOJ confirmed on Monday that Hayami had made such a statement in parliament last week, but a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  spokesman said he understood the governor to be conveying the views of Japanese companies, rather than his own opinion. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara also said the BOJ should communicate better with the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on its policy. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should also be more transparent about its Policy Board meetings, and the minutes of the meetings should be released more quickly and they should disclose specifically which board members said what, he added. \par \par \uc2  He also said it was time for the MOF to start releasing details of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  six months after each \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The market thinks joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is the only way to prevent the dollar's fall, but I think \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  alone would be effective," he said. \par \par \uc2  "I told (my successor Haruhiko) Kuroda that conducting $100 billion worth of dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the end of March would be effective, and said 'Why not do it?'," he added. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  received permission from the cabinet in November to raise more funds in the short-term money market to use to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  The ceiling on borrowing in the ministry's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  special account was lifted to 49 trillion yen from 39 trillion yen as part of the second supplementary budget for the fiscal year to next March. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara also said excessive declines in the dollar against the yen would damage the U.S. economy more than they would damage the Japanese economy. \par \par \uc2  He said there was no guarantee there would not be joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. "The market should be cautious (about joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) in December, when market liquidity is reduced. The market now fears a sudden fall in the dollar amid a thin market, but actually it is also a good chance (for the monetary authorities) to lift the dollar up." \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara also said he believed Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa would decisively defend the dollar against a fall below 100 yen. \par \par \uc2  On the recent fall in the euro below parity against the dollar, he said this was a short-term trend and the euro would strengthen in the medium to longer term. "I don't know by how much Japanese investors are cutting their losses by selling the euro but it would be very stupid for them to cut losses (on their euro holdings) at this point. If it were me, I would be very patient." \par \par \uc2  He said he was confident about the euro-zone economy because the information technology revolution, which had led the U.S. economy's growth, would start permeating in Japan and Europe. \par \par \uc2  On market perceptions about confusing comments by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Sakakibara said he saw it as a gap between the French and German approaches to making monetary policy, but he thought this would adjust gradually. \par \par \uc2  "What is more confusing is the Bank of Japan's (monetary policy-making). It's the worst." \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara said Hayami told parliament last week he was comfortable with the current dollar/yen rate. "That is a total mistake...someone who cannot be responsible about he says should step down from his position." \par \par \uc2  The BOJ confirmed on Monday that Hayami had made such a statement in par