\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dlr slips back as Japan recovery seen intact. \b0\par\par\uc2 706 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Dezember 1999\par 04:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Dec 6 (Reuters) - The dollar zig-zagged against the yen on Monday morning, jumping sharply after the release of relatively weak Japanese growth figures, but then falling back as traders decided that Japan's economic recovery remained intact. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The greenback\tab   jumped to a high of 103.20 yen from around 102.65/75 in early trade on news that July-September gross domestic product fell 1.0 percent from the previous quarter, compared with economists' expectations of a 0.2 percent fall.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  But selling led by Japanese exporters, investors and interbank dealers dragged the dollar back to 102.41/49 by 0221 GMT. \par \par \uc2  The GDP data initially had traders believing that the pace of Japan's economic recovery might not be as fast as previously thought. \par \par \uc2  "The market knew that public investment would fall, but it turns out that capital expenditure, which was supposed to cover for that fall, was pretty weak," said a Japanese city bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  But the dollar bounced back after touching 103.20 yen, a key level which remained the year's low for about two months before the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  tumbled to a four-year low of 101.25 yen in late November. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said he was not surprised that GDP posted a contraction from the previous quarter, saying the bigger-than-expected drop was mainly due to an upward revision in the April-June figure. \par \par \uc2  Japan's April-June real GDP was revised to plus 1.0 percent from plus 0.1 percent. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's slide gained momentum on sell-orders by Japanese exporters lined up near 103.50 yen, as well as hedge-selling by Japanese investors, dealers said. Selling was also detected by interbank dealers who had bought after the weaker-than-expected GDP data this morning. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said another reason the reaction to the GDP figure was shortlived was that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) had not conducted dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after its release of the data, as had been expected by some players. \par \par \uc2  "This may mean that the authorities have begun considering levels around 102-103 yen to the dollar as acceptable," the city bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Mitsumaru Kumagai, senior economist at the Industrial Bank of Japan, said the BOJ seemed to be taking a wait-and-see stance. \par \par \uc2  "One reason the BOJ did not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  might be that such a move could trigger unnecessary (dollar) buying to around 105 yen, and the BOJ did not want to (deliberately) induce such price fluctuations." \par \par \uc2  But dealers said wariness about \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had kept the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from tumbling to the 101-102 yen range. \par \par \uc2  Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Haruhiko Kuroda said on Monday the yen was too strong and had to be corrected. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The euro\tab      was quoted at 102.53/69 yen pressured by Japanese exporters' selling at around 103.50 yen by 0248 GMT. Dealers said the euro fell to a new life low of 102.18 yen in New York on Friday before closing at 102.90.\par Against the dollar, the euro\tab   stood at $1.0014/21 against $1.0022 in late U.S. trade, supported by heavy bids at around $0.9980, dealers said. ((Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432 1396 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)).\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bid prices at 0251GMT.  All data taken from Reuters\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par with percentage change calculated from the daily U.S. close at\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 2130GMT.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab \tab      Last  US Close  % Chg   YTD %   1998 Close\par \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab 03 Dec.\par \tab \tab \tab      -----------------------------------------\par  Euro/dlr\tab \tab     1.0016   1.0022   -0.06   -14.55   1.1721\par  Dlr/yen\tab \tab      102.39   102.67   -0.27   -14.55   113.20\par  Euro/yen\tab \tab     102.56   102.90   -0.33    -9.55   131.84\par  Dlr/swiss\tab \tab    1.5920   1.5909   +0.07   +15.91   1.3735\par  Stg/dlr\tab \tab      1.6010   1.6000   +0.06    -3.20   1.6539\par  Can/dlr\tab \tab      1.4793   1.4793   +0.00    -3.28   1.5295\par  Aus/dlr\tab \tab      0.6330   0.6319   +0.17    +3.52   0.6115\par  Euro/swiss\tab \tab   1.5951   1.5946   +0.03    -1.04   1.6119\par  Euro/stg\tab \tab     0.6254   0.6257   -0.05   -10.92   0.7021.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1999.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dvc608pda\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
