\par\par \b \uc2  Euro Slide Throws Japan Investors Into Painful Dilemma \b0\par\par\uc2  By Nick Shindo and Chang-Ran Kim \par \uc2 1,095 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Dezember 1999\par 11:48\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- The euro's slide to a record low against the dollar and yen has put Japanese investors and exporters in a serious bind: Do they sell their huge euro holdings now or wait for a recovery? \par \par \uc2  In a reversal of euro-phoria when the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debuted Jan. 3, sentiment toward the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has soured on concern over the pace of recovery in Germany and a significant repatriation of funds to the U.S. on rebounding confidence in U.S. asset markets, traders and analysts say. \par \par \uc2  The German economy is pivotal because it makes up roughly a third of total euro-zone economic activity. \par \par \uc2  With confidence crumbling around it, the euro has tumbled almost 15% against the dollar since the start of the year, dipping under parity to an all-time low of $0.9995 in New York Thursday. Against the yen it fell almost 25% to a record low of Y102.30 Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Japanese institutional investors and exporters are seriously wounded in the cross fire, dealers say. \par \par \uc2  Investment houses, which bought euros heavily earlier in the year, are said to have poorly hedged their euro long positions against downside risk. \par \par \uc2  Similarly, exporters are plagued with their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  losses, as well as the fall in sales to the European Union, which has slashed profits in yen terms. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say the lack of historical reference for the euro is among the multitude of problems of investing in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , leaving euro players frustrated and up in arms. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par        Lack of Hedging Mechanisms A Serious Problem\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The continued absence of sufficient hedging mechanisms "is a serious\par problem", says Shuji Takano, assistant \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  manager at\par ABN-Amro Bank in Tokyo.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A survey published Friday by Nikkei News Service says some Japanese\par institutional investors need the euro to rise above Y120 in order to\par erase unrealized losses on securities holdings.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Late Friday in Asia, the euro was around Y102.45.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Sumitomo Life Insurance Co. (J.SLI) needs the euro to recover to Y122,\par while Asahi Mutual Life Insurance Co. (J.AML) needs a further\par retracement to Y130, Nikkei says.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But institutional investors aren't ready to jump ship yet.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par One official at a major life insurer says, "Although we are keeping a\par close watch on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  levels, we have no immediate plans to change our\par euro-based asset allocation."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But exporters are starting to feel the heat, analysts said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Unrealized euro losses among Japanese exporters are climbing every\par day..., and there is a huge amount of nervousness among Japanese\par investors," Takano says.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Takano estimates that exporters have on average calculated the cost of\par holding euros against yen based on a rate of around Y128 and unrealized\par losses are dangerously high as the euro hovers about Y26 below that\par level.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par While such investors fret over whether or how long they should hold\par their euros, downward pressure isn't easing as other international\par players are also scrambling to square their positions, market watchers\par say.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "All other players with exposure to the euro are being forced to sell\par to cut losses," says Hidenori Watanabe, assistant general manager of\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at Dai-Ichi Kangyo Bank.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Others are more direct in their pessimism: "Parity (to the dollar) was\par the first target. Now the next target is for the dollar to break Y100,"\par says Ryohei Muramatsu, Treasury manager at Commerzbank in Tokyo.\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Could Lend Euro Some Support\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Among this doom and gloom there, may be a tinge of hope for euro-long\par players.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Against some market expectations, the euro didn't go into freefall\par against the dollar after breaking below parity, which indicates some\par resistance is propping up the downside.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Analysts say the main deterrent preventing a large euro sell-off and\par halting a precipitous drop against the dollar and yen is a slightly\par reluctant one: the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par While ECB officials have feigned apathy toward a weak euro and denied\par the possibility of euro-boosting \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , Sadaaki Tsunakawa, senior\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  analyst at Citibank, says the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will have to\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at some point lest its fledgling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  lose face\par permanently.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Everyone knows there will be \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . It's just the timing\par they're unsure about," Tsunakawa says, adding that coordinated\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  between the ECB and Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is "entirely possible."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Moreover, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the ECB, unlike that of the BOJ, would have\par the advantage of taking market players more by surprise.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Although it may not lead to a full-fledged euro recovery because of\par the huge amount of euro holdings that still need to be abolished, it\par could support the euro by raising the possibility in the market's mind\par of the U.S. joining in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  gala.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "If it's an \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to help the euro and the yen, (U.S. Treasury\par Secretary Lawrence) Summers won't face that much opposition in\par Congress," Tsunakawa says.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Full-Fledged Euro Recovery May Be Some Way Off\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Analysts say even coordinated \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  wouldn't be able to\par obliterate the reality of unhedged euro positions, which will likely\par take time to clear.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Euro-long positions won't be squared that easily. So any real\par recovery in the euro would take another half a year or so," says\par Tsunakawa.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par One yardstick investors are watching is the performance of the\par Japanese economy compared with the German one, which will become clearer\par next week.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par If Germany does better than Japan in terms of market expectations,\par that could prompt euro holders to wait and hope for a recovery in the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  before they square.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On the other hand, if Japan's economic growth proves stronger than\par what most expect, it would only give investors more impetus to sell the\par euro further, eventually knocking it down to around Y95 within a few\par months.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "That's only seven yen away; it's nothing," says Citibank's Tsunakawa.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Even if Germany comes out on top, the euro isn't expected to win much\par favor from Japanese players after the harsh beating they got during its\par first year.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I think investors are fed up, and next year, even if the euro starts\par looking up, people won't feel much like reinvesting in it," says\par Hidenori Watanabe, assistant general manager of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at\par Dai-Ichi Kangyo Bank. "I think people feel that it was a mistake to\par invest in the euro at all."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Nick Shindo; 813-5255-2936; nick.shindo@dowjones.com, and\par Chang-Ran Kim; 813-5255-2933; chang-ran.kim@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1134 4202 55127 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dvc3089tt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
