\par\par \b \uc2  Japan insurers to stick with euro despite parity. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Masayuki Kitano \par \uc2 700 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Dezember 1999\par 08:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Dec 3 (Reuters) - Major Japanese life insurers are not about to dump their euro-denominated assets despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's fall below parity with the dollar for the first time, company officials said on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "I think the euro will stop falling at some point, especially because the prospects for growth in the euro zone next year are strong," said a senior official in Nippon Life Insurance Co's international investment division. \par \par \uc2  Nippon Life held more than 1.4 trillion yen in euro-denominated assets at the end of September and the official said that, for the time being, it did not intend to cut back. \par \par \uc2  With markets growing thinner as the year-end approaches, analysts agreed that major investors would be reluctant to bale out of the euro. \par \par \uc2  "The larger Japanese investors will probably refrain from actively unloading their euro-denominated assets, as that would be akin to tightening their own noose," said Akio Makabe, chief economist for Dai-Ichi Kangyo Research. \par \par \uc2  "But smaller investors with less tolerance for risk may have no choice but to gradually cut back," Makabe said. \par \par \uc2  Traders said sales of euro assets could accelerate in the new year, especially as the time comes to close the books on the fiscal year at the end of March. \par \par \uc2  The risk of some investor selling, coupled with the low probability that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  alongside the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell yen, means the euro could still fall below 100 yen before the year-end, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The euro was trading in Tokyo around the day's low of 102.38 yen late on Friday and fell to a fresh low against the dollar of $0.9990. \par \par \uc2  European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Wim Duisenberg was quoted by The Wall Street Journal's Internet edition on Friday as saying the ECB would never \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  with \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  alone to curb the yen's rise. He also said he did not expect joint U.S.-\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 -ECB \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets at the moment. \par \par \uc2  "The ECB's view seems to be that the euro's fall has not been so great that it is hindering their conduct of monetary policy," said the Nippon Life official. \par \par \uc2  JAPANESE PUNISHED FOR THEIR EURO-ENTHUSIASM \par \par \uc2  The euro has lost 15 percent against the dollar since its launch in January. But it has fallen even harder against the yen, losing 23 percent of it value. On Tuesday it plumbed a low of 102.23 yen. \par \par \uc2  The slide is galling for Japanese life insurers, which jumped headlong into the euro and are now being punished for their enthusiasm. \par \par \uc2  The country's six largest life insurers and nine second-tier life insurers held 4.3 trillion yen worth of euro-denominated assets at the end of September, up from 3.7 trillion yen at the end of March. \par \par \uc2  Mitsumaru Kumagai, senior market analyst for the Industrial Bank of Japan, said Japanese investors' losses from their investments in German and French securities alone since April 1998 are estimated at roughly 1.5 trillion yen. \par \par \uc2  The estimate assumes a hedge ratio of 40 percent. \par \par \uc2  "Speculators are using Japanese investors' losses from their euro assets as a target," betting that further declines in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will force them to cut their losses, Kumagai said. \par \par \uc2  Despite the short-term pain, officials at major life insurers said the euro's break below parity would not significantly alter their investment plans. \par \par \uc2  "I feel lucky to have been able to witness such a historic event," said an official for Dai-Ichi Life Insurance Co's securities investment department. "But this isn't going to influence our investment one way or another." \par \par \uc2  An official at Mitsui Mutual Life Insurance Co's international fixed-income group said the euro's fall would have a miniscule impact as Mitsui has almost fully hedged its exposure to foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk. \par \par \uc2  The euro's decline will not by itself be a reason for Mitsui Mutual to reduce its holdings of euro-denominated assets, he said. ((Masayuki Kitano, Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 5473 3748 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dvc308wou\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
