\par\par \b \uc2  Hard for U.S. to join yen-selling  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  - BOJ. \b0\par\par\uc2 292 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Dezember 1999\par 08:06\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Dec 3 (Reuters) - Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Masaru Hayami told parliament on Friday it would be difficult for the United States to join Tokyo in selling yen for dollars given the huge U.S. current account deficit. \par \par \uc2  Answering questions in the Lower House of parliament's Finance Committee, Hayami noted that the strong U.S. economy, with its appetite for imports, has ballooned the nation's current account deficit to some four percent of gross domestic product. \par \par \uc2  "In such a situation, I think it would be difficult for the U.S. to join a yen-selling, dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Hayami said. \par \par \uc2  At the same time, Hayami said an excessively strong yen was detrimental to Japan's economy, and hurt Japanese corporate profits. \par \par \uc2  Asked about the euro's recent slide versus the dollar, Hayami said he did not think that Europe's monetary authorities were overly concerned about the euro's drop in value. \par \par \uc2  "It doesn't seem that European authorities are excessively concerned (about the euro's fall)," Hayami said. \par \par \uc2  "Instead, it seems that they are paying more attention to (domestic) price stability." \par \par \uc2  Hayami also said the yen's recent strength was not causing excessive concern overseas, including Asian nations. \par \par \uc2  Pressed on whether the BOJ had been changing the direction of its monetary policy since September 21, when its regular policy-setting meeting rebuffed pressure to ease monetary policy in order to support \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, Hayami said: "The policy of the Bank of Japan remains unchanged." \par \par \uc2  He added that he believed the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's stance was consistent with the Finance Ministry's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. ((Tokyo newsroom (813) 3432-8022 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dvc308wj0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
