\par\par \b \uc2  Duisenberg - Won't act on yen with Japan alone-WSJ. \b0\par\par\uc2 718 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Dezember 1999\par 07:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Dec 3 (Reuters) - European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Wim Duisenberg said in an interview published on Friday by the Wall Street Journal that the ECB would never \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  with \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  alone to curb the yen's rise. \par \par \uc2  In its Internet edition, the newspaper quoted Duisenberg as saying in an interview earlier this week that he did not expect joint U.S.-\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 -ECB \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the moment. \par \par \uc2  Duisenberg said he thought the euro's fall was overdone but he was not concerned about it, and said the only significance of the euro falling to parity at $1.00 - a level it breached early on Friday - was psychological. \par \par \uc2  He said the euro's weakness was not increasing the risk of euro-zone inflation and the yen's strength was not posing a danger to the global economy. \par \par \uc2  Asked if there were circumstances under which the ECB would join \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen, Duisenberg said: "No, never with \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  alone. There are circumstances where there could be coordinated action between the three big central banks, but I don't foresee that at this time." \par \par \uc2  While playing down the threat of the strong yen, he said he had "some understanding for the concern that the Japanese authorities have, that the strength of the yen might stifle the performance of the Japanese economy". \par \par \uc2  Duisenberg said he thought the euro's fall was overdone but he was not concerned about it, and said the only significance of the euro falling to parity at $1.00 - a level it breached early on Friday - was psychological. \par \par \uc2  He said the euro's weakness was not increasing the risk of euro-zone inflation and the yen's strength was not posing a danger to the global economy. \par \par \uc2  Asked if there were circumstances under which the ECB would join \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen, Duisenberg said: "No, never with \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  alone. There are circumstances where there could be coordinated action between the three big central banks, but I don't foresee that at this time." \par \par \uc2  While playing down the threat of the strong yen, he said he had "some understanding for the concern that the Japanese authorities have, that the strength of the yen might stifle the performance of the Japanese economy". \par \par \uc2  Duisenberg's comments contrast with those of Japanese offficials, who have repeatedly said a September Group of Seven statement - which stated the other nations share Japan's concern about the effects of the yen's rise on the global economy - was still in force. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets this week, selling yen for dollars and euros, and a senior Japanese official reiterated on Friday that Tokyo thinks the yen is too strong against the dollar and the euro and that the euro is too weak against the dollar and the yen. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Asked if there was a level for the euro which would establish confidence in the markets, Duisenberg said he would not be surprised if the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  moved back to the $1.08-$1.10\tab   level, but added that the ECB does not have a target exchange rate.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par He said he was disappointed by the euro's fall but said it did not worry him.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I won't deny that what I regard as the overshooting of the downward direction of the exchange rate is a psychological disappointment, although it doesn't give me cause for concern, and it doesn't worry me," the newspaper quoted Duisenberg as saying.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On the European economy, Duisenberg said a big problem facing it was the German economy's slow and lagging performance, which he said was confirmed by events such as the government rescue of Holzmann AG  and its comments on Mannesmann AG .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "In my view they reflect more the past than a future-oriented dynamic economy," he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Duisenberg said he had doubts as to whether these events in Germany constituted wise economic policy.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "It does demonstrate that the dynamics which you would expect from an economy have not arrived here," he said, adding that these events were not new but happened time and again in various other countries as well. ((Tokyo Newsroom +81-3 3432 8022 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1999.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dvc308wgv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
