\par\par \b \uc2  Yen remains too strong vs dlr,euro - MOF's Mizoguchi. \b0\par\par\uc2 232 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Dezember 1999\par 01:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Dec 3 (Reuters) - Senior Finance Ministry official Zembei Mizoguchi said on Friday the yen remains too strong against both the euro and the dollar and that Japan will act as appropriate on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  conditions. \par \par \uc2  Asked about the euro's drop below $1 for the first time in New York trade overnight, Mizoguchi told reporters: "There is no change in our policy of continuing to watch market conditions closely and responding as appropriate." \par \par \uc2  Asked whether the yen was too strong against both the dollar and the euro, he said: "Yes, as I have said before, the yen's rise since this summer has been inappropriate." \par \par \uc2  Asked if the MOF was now more concerned about the yen's rise against the euro than against the dollar, Mizoguchi said the ministry was watching both pairs. \par \par \uc2  On whether joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was the only way to address the weak euro and strong yen, Mizoguchi said: "There are many views in the market, so it's not as simple as that. The yen's rise against both the dollar and the euro is not desirable, and the only thing to do is to closely watch market conditions and respond as appropriate." ((William Mallard, Tokyo newsroom +81-3 3432-8022 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dvc308ve8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
