\par\par \b \uc2  World economic prospects getting brighter, IMF says. \b0\par\par\uc2 451 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Dezember 1999\par 22:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WASHINGTON, Dec 2 (Reuters) - World economic prospects have brightened considerably, but there is no room for complacency as countries emerge from the crisis of 1997-99, the deputy head of the International Monetary Fund said on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  IMF First Deputy Managing Director Stanley Fischer told a conference that both Asia and Latin America were recovering from the crisis, which started in Thailand in the summer of 1997 and spread relentlessly around the world. \par \par \uc2  "The outlook is brighter than it has been for some time but - you can rely on someone from the IMF to say this - there is no cause for complacency," Fischer told a conference in Washington, according to a text of his remarks released ahead of delivery. \par \par \uc2  Fischer, noting growth forecasts of 9 percent for South Korea and 3-4 percent for Thailand, said sound policies and the introduction of structural reforms were behind the return to growth across Asia. \par \par \uc2  Output was set to expand in Brazil and Mexico, while Argentina's deep recession appeared to be bottoming out. There was no need for a return to the "indiscriminate capital flows" of the middle of 1997, but improved investment conditions for emerging markets could help the recovery there. \par \par \uc2  Fischer said Japan's return to growth was helping the economic recovery elsewhere in Asia. But monetary policy needed to become even more expansionary until this recovery was guaranteed and the risk of deflation had eased. \par \par \uc2  "Monetary policy in Japan should remain expansionary, indeed it should become more expansionary, until the recovery there is on firmer ground and remaining deflationary risks have passed," he said. \par \par \uc2  Japanese interest rates are already virtually at zero, which means there is little room to expand monetary policy by conventional means. \par \par \uc2  Flemming Larsen, the IMF's deputy chief economist, said last week that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could consider unsterilized \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem an unwelcome appreciation of the yen. \par \par \uc2  Central banks normally "sterilize" \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by mopping up the money they have pumped into the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, thereby neutralising the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  effects on money supply. \par \par \uc2  By leaving yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  unsterilized, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would expand the money supply and in theory put further downward pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Fischer also welcomed last month's U.S. interest rate rise, when the Federal Reserve raised key interest rates by 25 basis points. \par \par \uc2  "As we say year after year, a slowdown in U.S. growth is inevitable," Fischer said. \par \par \uc2  "The modest tightening of monetary policy put in place by the Federal Reserve is a welcome response to potential inflationary pressures and it should help ensure that this remarkable upswing continues at a sustainable pace." \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dvc208v6u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
