\par\par \b \uc2  Japan needs "more expansionary" money policy-IMF. \b0\par\par\uc2 310 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Dezember 1999\par 22:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WASHINGTON, Dec 2 (Reuters) - Japan will need an even more expansionary monetary policy until its economic recovery is guaranteed and the risk of deflation has eased, the deputy head of the International Monetary Fund said on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  IMF First Deputy Managing Director Stanley Fischer said Japan's return to growth was helping the economic recovery elsewhere in Asia. But monetary policy needed to stay easy. \par \par \uc2  "Monetary policy in Japan should remain expansionary, indeed it should become more expansionary, until the recovery there is on firmer ground and remaining deflationary risks have passed," he told a conference in Washington, according to a text of his remarks released ahead of delivery. \par \par \uc2  Japanese interest rates are already virtually at zero, which means there is little room to expand monetary policy by conventional means. \par \par \uc2  Flemming Larsen, the IMF's deputy chief economist, said last week that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could consider unsterilized \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem an unwelcome appreciation of the yen. \par \par \uc2  Central banks normally "sterilize" \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by mopping up the money they have pumped into the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, thereby neutralising the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  effects on money supply. \par \par \uc2  By leaving yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  unsterilized, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would expand the money supply and in theory put further downward pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Fischer also welcomed last month's U.S. interest rate rise, when the Federal Reserve raised key interest rates by 25 basis points. \par \par \uc2  "As we say year after year, a slowdown in U.S. growth is inevitable," Fischer said. "The modest tightening of monetary policy put in place by the Federal Reserve is a welcome response to potential inflationary pressures and it should help ensure that this remarkable upswing continues at a sustainable pace." \par \par \uc2  ((Janet Guttsman, Washington newsroom, +1 202 898 8309 washington.economic.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dvc208v6t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
