\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Up Vs Yen On Talk BOJ May  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2  Again \b0\par\par\uc2 729 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Dezember 1999\par 21:40\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  By Umberto Torresan \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)-  The dollar was trading higher against the yen and the euro late Wednesday in New York as players consolidated their positions amid talk of further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese authorities to slow the rise of the yen. \par \par \uc2  At about 3:30 p.m. EST (2030 GMT), the dollar was trading at Y102.72, up from Y102.10 late Tuesday in New York. The euro was trading at $1.0083, down from $1.0105 earlier in London but nearly flat from $1.0091 late Tuesday in New York. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market had fairly quiet day Wednesday, with the dollar hovering around the Y102.20 level for most of the session after climbing as high as Y102.75 intraday. \par \par \uc2  The move higher was fueled by Bank of Japan's Governor Masaru Hayami, who hinted at the possibility that the bank would leave future \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  "unsterilized." \par \par \uc2  Unsterilized \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  implies leaving the extra yen resulting from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the banking system in a bid to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Under current practice, the BOJ conducts special market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  to recapture yen released into the market through \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Hayami issued a statement stating the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  strongly hopes for an early stabilization of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates and declaring the BOJ supports \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. \par \par \uc2  But traders and analysts downplayed the statement, concluding BOJ policy hadn't changed and predicting pressure on the dollar will continue. \par \par \uc2  However, market talk about increasing odds for a joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  between the U.S. Treasury and the BOJ to slow the rise of the yen was enough to damp the yen's gains against the dollar in late trading. \par \par \uc2  The euro rose to an intraday high of $1.0128 midmorning in London, only to come crashing back down under $1.01 in New York trading, to end the session little changed from where it started. Strong stop loss orders and option orders just above $1.0000 are likely to discourage an imminent incursion below parity levels, analysts say. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, sterling posted modest gains against the euro and the dollar Wednesday after a European morning session in which neither support nor resistance levels were tested. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at GBP0.6299, down from GBP0.6312 late Tuesday in New York. Sterling was quoted at $1.6010, up from $1.5985 late Tuesday. The pound was lightly traded against the euro for most of the European day, unable to break out of a 0.15-pence range. \par \par \uc2  "The range is really narrow and there's no real momentum to this market," said Robin Poynder, chief dealer at Credit Commercial de France. \par \par \uc2  Poynder said the euro has support at GBP0.6275-80 and resistance at GBP0.6330. \par \par \uc2  The pound was also quiet against the dollar, trading one-third of a cent on either side of $1.6000 for most of the session. \par \par \uc2  The Swiss franc continued to recover late in European trading from its losses against the dollar earlier this week and last week. \par \par \uc2  "There is some profit-taking going on again," said a trader with a Swiss bank. "However, if the euro starts to weaken again, the dollar will also move higher against the Swiss franc." \par \par \uc2  Late in New York, the dollar did gain ground against the franc, trading at CHF1.5881 from CHF1.5870 late Tuesday, while the euro was at CHF1.6014, nearly unchanged from CHF1.6017. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthened Wednesday, recovering some of the ground lost in Tuesday's fall as short-term market players were forced to cut back their long positions in the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  In Southeast Asia the baht, rupiah, Singapore dollar and Philippine peso all ended Asian trading hours higher on the day. In North Asia solid investment inflows continued to support the South Korean won and the New Taiwan dollar. \par \par \uc2  Despite the regional rally Wednesday, many analysts maintained their view that Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will remain vulnerable to selling pressure through December. \par \par \uc2  "The fears over Y2K may be overdone, but the concern will remain at the back of traders' minds until the end of the year, and corporate customers will still want to buy dollars," warned Andrew Fung, treasury economist at Standard Chartered Bank in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  (Gonzalo Vina in London and Tom Holland in Hong Kong contributed to this article.) \par \par \uc2  1074 4164 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dvc10894m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
